Key engineering details you implemented:

Streaming cloud-optimized GeoTIFFs (COGs) directly from S3 via GDAL /vsis3/ + rasterio — no full-file download. See detection_common.py.
Geospatial correctness: pixel-area-to-square-meters conversion handling both projected and geographic CRS; tiling large rasters into overlapping windows; converting pixel detections back to geo-coordinates.
Classical CV post-processing: OpenCV HSV color filtering, contour extraction, Non-Max Suppression to dedupe overlapping detections across tiles.
Shared service refactor: extracted common S3/GDAL/raster helpers into detection_common.py so each detector stays focused on its algorithm.
2. Backend API endpoints
Built REST endpoints for each detector (tree_detection.py, building_detection.py, stockpile_detection.py) following an async job pattern: POST /detect enqueues a Celery task and returns a task ID; client polls a status endpoint; results returned when ready.
Celery + Redis background task orchestration with per-task progress callbacks (tree_tasks.py, building_tasks.py, stockpile_tasks.py) and dedicated task queues.
Task ownership & RBAC: stored task owner in Redis and enforced owner-or-admin access; reused shared auth/collection-access dependencies (detection.py).
Pydantic v2 schemas for request/response validation (tree.py, building.py, stockpile.py).
Alembic migrations adding new AssetType enum values for detection outputs; added an order_types enum (7e47aa0).
