What you should learn next
Honest gaps based on what the code shows:

Automated testing — the repo has no tests (CLAUDE.md notes "not yet implemented"). Learn pytest, FastAPI's TestClient, and mocking S3/Celery. This is the single biggest credibility gap for a backend engineer.
ML model serving & optimization — you're loading heavy models (SAM-2/DeepForest) per task. Learn model warm-loading, batching, GPU memory management, and tools like TorchServe / Triton / ONNX Runtime.
Async Python depth — your endpoints are sync (def, not async def). Learn async/await, when it helps, and async SQLAlchemy.
Observability — structured logging, metrics (Prometheus/Grafana), and distributed tracing for long-running Celery jobs.
MLOps — model versioning/registries (MLflow), reproducible inference, and evaluating detection accuracy (precision/recall/mAP) rather than relying on heuristic filters.
Infra-as-code — you used systemd + manual deploy scripts; learning Kubernetes or at least docker-compose orchestration for the worker fleet would round this out.
